# {{module}}

A static showcase site built with [Airway](https://github.com/daqing/airway)
and its corporate theme — pure HTML and CSS output, deployable to any static
host (GitHub Pages, Netlify, object storage + CDN, ...).

## How it works

`ssg.go` in the project root defines the site: site metadata, the theme,
and every page. It registers a builder with `cmd.SetSSGBuilder`, which the
`airway ssg:build` and `airway ssg:serve` commands run — no database, no
Node toolchain, no running server required.

## Common commands

```bash
airway ssg:build             # export the static site into dist/
airway ssg:serve             # preview on http://127.0.0.1:3000
airway ssg:serve --addr :8080
```

Both commands run on whatever the project's `ssg.go` defines. Inside this
project, a globally installed `airway` proxies to `go run .` automatically,
so both forms are equivalent.

## Editing pages

Each `s.Page(slug, title, data)` call in `ssg.go` is one output page; the
slug decides the export path (`/about` becomes `dist/about/index.html`). The
corporate theme understands these data types:

- `corporate.Home` — the landing page: hero heading, tagline, feature grid
- `corporate.Content` — a plain text page: lead plus paragraphs
- a `templ.Component` — a custom body rendered inside the shared layout

The full guide, including how to build your own theme, lives in
[docs/ssg.md](https://github.com/daqing/airway/blob/main/docs/ssg.md).

## Deployment

`airway ssg:build` writes a self-contained static directory into `dist/`:
one `index.html` per page plus the theme's `assets/`. Upload the directory
to any static host. The site is rebuildable from the repository alone:
`go run . ssg:build` needs only Go and the module cache.
